-- Q1: medium / L2 — 401(k) loan rules (max amount)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Thornfield Engineering employee Priya Desai has a vested 401(k) balance of $90,000. She requests a plan loan. Under IRC Section 72(p), what is the maximum loan amount Priya can receive?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$45,000","$50,000","$90,000","$70,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 72(p)(2)(A) limits 401(k) loans to the lesser of $50,000 or 50% of the participant's vested account balance. Since 50% of $90,000 is $45,000, which is less than the $50,000 cap, Priya's maximum loan is $45,000. Wrong (B): $50,000 is the absolute cap but only applies when 50% of the vested balance exceeds $50,000 (i.e., balances over $100,000). Wrong (C): The full balance is never available as a loan; the 50% limitation always applies. Wrong (D): $70,000 exceeds both the 50% and absolute dollar limits.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — QDRO distribution and penalty exception
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$During a divorce, a court issues a qualified domestic relations order (QDRO) awarding $60,000 of Marcus Petrov's 401(k) to his ex-spouse, Nadia. Nadia, age 38, takes a lump-sum distribution from the plan. What is the tax treatment of her distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Taxable as ordinary income with a 10% early withdrawal penalty","Tax-free because QDROs are not taxable events","Taxable as ordinary income with no early withdrawal penalty","Taxable as capital gain with no penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 72(t)(2)(C) exempts QDRO distributions from employer plans from the 10% early withdrawal penalty. However, the distribution is still included in the recipient's gross income as ordinary income under Section 402(e)(1). Wrong (A): Although the distribution is taxable, the 10% penalty does not apply to QDRO distributions. Wrong (B): QDRO distributions are taxable to the alternate payee; only a rollover would defer tax. Wrong (D): Retirement plan distributions are taxed as ordinary income, not capital gains.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Excess IRA contribution 6% excise tax
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Accountant Rebecca Hartwell, age 44, has earned income of $5,000 in 2026. She contributes $7,000 to her Roth IRA. What is the tax consequence of the excess contribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A 10% penalty on the excess amount","A 6% excise tax of $120 per year until the excess is corrected","No penalty if corrected before the tax filing deadline","A 6% excise tax and ordinary income tax on the excess"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 4973, excess IRA contributions are subject to a 6% excise tax per year until withdrawn. Rebecca's contribution limit is capped at $5,000 (her earned income), making $2,000 the excess. The 6% tax on $2,000 is $120 annually. Wrong (A): The penalty rate for excess contributions is 6%, not 10%. Wrong (C): While excess contributions can be corrected by the filing deadline (plus extensions) to avoid the penalty for that year, the question states no such correction. Wrong (D): The excess amount itself is not subject to ordinary income tax upon contribution; only the 6% excise applies.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — 60-day rollover rule vs direct trustee-to-trustee
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Financial advisor Craig Nguyen's client, age 50, receives a $100,000 distribution check from her former employer's 401(k). The employer withheld 20% for federal taxes. The client wants to complete a tax-free rollover to her new IRA. Craig must determine what amount she needs to deposit into the IRA within 60 days and the consequences if she falls short.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["She must deposit $80,000 (the net check) to complete a full rollover","She must deposit $100,000 within 60 days; the $20,000 shortfall is taxable income if not replaced","She can deposit only $80,000, and the $20,000 withholding is automatically treated as rolled over","She has 90 days to deposit $80,000 under SECURE 2.0 extended rollover rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 402(c), to complete a tax-free rollover of the full distribution, the participant must deposit $100,000 into the IRA within 60 days — including replacing the $20,000 withheld from personal funds. Any shortfall is treated as a taxable distribution and may be subject to the 10% early withdrawal penalty. Wrong (A): Depositing only $80,000 means $20,000 is treated as a taxable distribution, not a complete rollover. Wrong (C): The withheld amount is not automatically treated as rolled over; the participant must make up the difference. Wrong (D): The rollover deadline remains 60 days; SECURE 2.0 did not extend it to 90 days for standard rollovers.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — ADP nondiscrimination testing corrective distribution
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Meridian Consulting's 401(k) plan fails the ADP test for 2026. The average deferral percentage for highly compensated employees (HCEs) is 8%, while non-HCEs average 4%. The plan uses the basic test under IRC Section 401(k)(3). The plan administrator must evaluate corrective options. Which corrective action satisfies the nondiscrimination requirement while minimizing employee disruption?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Distribute excess contributions to HCEs by March 15 of the following year plus allocable earnings","Reduce all employees' deferral rates to 4% prospectively","Terminate the plan and distribute all balances","Retroactively increase NHCE contribution rates to match HCEs"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 401(k)(8), excess contributions to HCEs may be corrected by distributing the excess plus allocable income by the 2.5-month deadline (March 15 following the plan year). This targeted correction satisfies the ADP test without affecting NHCEs. Wrong (B): Reducing all deferral rates is unnecessarily disruptive and penalizes NHCEs who are already compliant. Wrong (C): Plan termination is an extreme measure not required by ADP test failure. Wrong (D): The plan cannot retroactively force NHCEs to contribute more; contributions must be voluntary.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Saver's Credit (Retirement Savings Contributions Credit)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Married couple David and Kira Okafor file jointly with AGI of $46,000 in 2026. David contributes $2,500 to his 401(k) and Kira contributes $2,000 to her Roth IRA. They claim the Saver's Credit under IRC Section 25B. At the 20% credit rate, what is their total Saver's Credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$800","$500","$1,000","$4,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 25B, the Saver's Credit applies to the first $2,000 of eligible retirement contributions per individual. David's credit base is $2,000 (capped from $2,500), and Kira's is $2,000. Combined eligible amount: $4,000. At the 20% rate: $4,000 x 20% = $800. Wrong (B): $500 would result from applying the wrong rate or incorrect contribution caps. Wrong (C): $1,000 would result from applying a 50% credit rate, which applies only at lower income levels. Wrong (D): $4,000 is the total eligible contribution base, not the credit amount; the credit is a percentage of the base.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — 529 superfunding (5-year gift tax election)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Grandparent Helen Whitmore contributes $95,000 to a 529 plan for her grandchild in 2026. She elects the 5-year gift tax averaging under IRC Section 529(c)(2)(B). The annual gift tax exclusion is $19,000. Helen makes no other gifts to the grandchild during the 5-year period but passes away in year 3. What is the estate tax consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No amount is included in Helen's estate because the full election was made","$38,000 is included in Helen's estate for the two remaining years","$95,000 is included because the election is voided upon death","$57,000 is included for the three remaining years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 529(c)(4)(C), if the donor dies during the 5-year averaging period, the portion allocable to the remaining calendar years after the year of death is included in the gross estate. Helen elected $19,000 x 5 = $95,000 over 5 years. If she dies in year 3, years 4 and 5 are remaining: $19,000 x 2 = $38,000 is included in her estate. Wrong (A): The election does not fully remove the contribution from the estate if the donor dies before the 5-year period ends. Wrong (C): The election is not voided; only the remaining years' allocable portion is recaptured. Wrong (D): $57,000 would represent 3 years, but year 3 (the year of death) is not included — only years 4 and 5 are recaptured.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Safe harbor 401(k) avoids ADP testing
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Janet Blake advises Pinecrest Staffing, which has 200 employees and consistently fails 401(k) nondiscrimination testing. Top-heavy rules require minimum contributions, and corrective distributions reduce HCE morale. Janet evaluates converting to a safe harbor 401(k) plan under IRC Section 401(k)(12). Pinecrest's average NHCE compensation is $55,000. Assess whether the safe harbor design is appropriate and identify the required employer cost.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Safe harbor basic match: 100% on first 3% plus 50% on next 2% of compensation, satisfying ADP/ACP safe harbor","Safe harbor requires 6% nonelective contribution to all eligible employees","Safe harbor eliminates ADP testing but doubles the employer's PBGC insurance premiums","Safe harbor match of 50% on the first 6% of compensation qualifies as the basic matching formula"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 401(k)(12)(B) defines the basic safe harbor match as 100% on the first 3% of compensation plus 50% on the next 2%. This formula automatically satisfies ADP and ACP nondiscrimination tests. The match must be immediately 100% vested. Wrong (B): The nonelective safe harbor requires 3% of compensation (not 6%) for all eligible employees under Section 401(k)(12)(C). Wrong (C): 401(k) plans are defined contribution plans and are not covered by PBGC insurance; PBGC covers only defined benefit plans. Wrong (D): A 50% match on the first 6% is an enhanced match option but is not the basic matching formula defined in the statute.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — 457(b) deferred compensation plan characteristics
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$City of Lakeview employee Sharon Moss, age 48, participates in a governmental 457(b) deferred compensation plan. She also participates in the city's 401(a) defined contribution plan. In 2026, what is the maximum Sharon can defer into the 457(b) plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$23,500, the same as a 401(k) elective deferral limit","$16,500, the SIMPLE IRA limit","$7,000, the IRA annual limit","$47,000, combining the 457(b) and 401(a) limits"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 457(e)(15), the 457(b) deferral limit is the same as the 401(k) limit — $23,500 for 2026. Importantly, 457(b) deferrals are not aggregated with 401(k)/403(b) deferrals, so Sharon can contribute the full $23,500 to the 457(b) in addition to any contributions to the 401(a) plan. Wrong (B): $16,500 is the SIMPLE IRA deferral limit and does not apply to 457(b) plans. Wrong (C): $7,000 is the IRA contribution limit, not the 457(b) limit. Wrong (D): The 457(b) limit stands independently; it is not combined with the 401(a) plan's annual addition limit.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Profit-sharing plan discretionary contributions
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Ridgemont Industries maintains a profit-sharing plan for its employees. In a year when Ridgemont has no profits, the company asks its CPA whether it must still make plan contributions. What is the correct answer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, the employer must contribute at least 3% of compensation annually","No, profit-sharing plan contributions are discretionary and not required in any given year","Yes, but the employer can reduce contributions to 1% of compensation","No, the plan must be terminated if the employer has no profits"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Profit-sharing plans under IRC Section 401(a) allow employer contributions to be discretionary. The employer is not required to make contributions in any given year, regardless of profitability, as long as contributions are recurring and substantial over time. Wrong (A): There is no minimum annual contribution requirement for profit-sharing plans, unlike safe harbor 401(k) plans. Wrong (C): No mandatory reduced contribution rate exists; the employer may contribute zero. Wrong (D): A year without contributions does not require plan termination; plans must simply not be maintained solely for the purpose of discriminating in favor of HCEs.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Money purchase pension plan definition
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is a money purchase pension plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A plan where the employer promises a fixed retirement benefit based on a formula","A defined contribution plan requiring fixed annual employer contributions as a percentage of compensation","A plan funded exclusively by employee salary deferrals","A plan that guarantees a minimum investment return on contributions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A money purchase pension plan is a defined contribution plan under IRC Section 401(a) in which the employer must make mandatory annual contributions at a fixed percentage of each participant's compensation. Unlike profit-sharing plans, the contribution rate is not discretionary. Wrong (A): A fixed retirement benefit based on a formula describes a defined benefit plan, not a money purchase plan. Wrong (C): Money purchase plans are funded by employer contributions, not employee salary deferrals; salary deferral is a feature of 401(k) plans. Wrong (D): Money purchase plans do not guarantee investment returns; participants bear investment risk as in all defined contribution plans.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — 403(b) 15-year service special catch-up
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Professor Leonard Cho, age 48, has worked at Glendale University for 18 years and participates in the school's 403(b) plan. Over his career, Leonard has contributed an average of $12,000 per year when the limit was higher. Under IRC Section 402(g)(7), he qualifies for the 15-year service catch-up. The standard 2026 elective deferral limit is $23,500. Calculate the maximum Leonard can defer in 2026.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$23,500 — the standard limit applies regardless of service","$26,500 — standard limit plus $3,000 special catch-up","$31,000 — standard limit plus $7,500 age-50 catch-up","$27,000 — standard limit plus a partial special catch-up"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 402(g)(7), employees with 15+ years of service at qualified organizations may defer an additional $3,000 per year (lifetime cap of $15,000). Since Leonard is under age 50, only the 15-year catch-up applies: $23,500 + $3,000 = $26,500. The special catch-up is applied before the age-50 catch-up. Wrong (A): Leonard qualifies for the special 15-year catch-up, so the standard limit understates his maximum. Wrong (C): The $7,500 age-50 catch-up does not apply because Leonard is only 48. Wrong (D): The special catch-up is exactly $3,000 per year (not a partial amount), assuming lifetime usage has not reached $15,000.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L2 — SIMPLE IRA eligibility requirements
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Clearwater Dental establishes a SIMPLE IRA plan. Employee technician Jamal Price earned $4,200 in 2024 and $4,800 in 2025. Under IRC Section 408(p)(4), is Jamal eligible to participate in the SIMPLE IRA for 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because he earned more than $4,000 in each prior year","No, because he did not earn at least $5,000 in compensation in any 2 preceding years","Yes, because all full-time employees are automatically eligible","No, because he must have 3 consecutive years of $5,000 compensation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 408(p)(4)(A) requires that eligible employees must have received at least $5,000 in compensation during any 2 preceding calendar years. Jamal earned only $4,200 and $4,800 — neither year reached $5,000, so he does not meet the eligibility threshold. Wrong (A): The threshold is $5,000, not $4,000, per preceding year. Wrong (C): SIMPLE IRA eligibility is not automatic for all employees; the $5,000 compensation test applies. Wrong (D): The requirement is any 2 preceding years (not necessarily consecutive), and only 2 years are needed, not 3.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — ESOP characteristics
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is the primary characteristic of an employee stock ownership plan (ESOP)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It allows employees to defer salary into a tax-advantaged account","It invests primarily in the employer's own stock","It guarantees a fixed annual retirement benefit","It is available only to self-employed individuals"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): An ESOP is a qualified defined contribution plan under IRC Section 4975(e)(7) that is designed to invest primarily in qualifying employer securities. ESOPs provide employees with ownership interests in their employer. Wrong (A): Salary deferral is a feature of 401(k) plans, not a defining ESOP characteristic; ESOPs are typically funded by employer contributions. Wrong (C): Guaranteed fixed benefits describe defined benefit plans, not ESOPs (which are defined contribution plans). Wrong (D): ESOPs are employer-sponsored plans for employees; self-employed plans include SEP-IRAs and solo 401(k)s.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — In-service distribution from 401(k) at age 59½
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Regional manager Tanya Fielding, age 61, is still employed at Briarwood Enterprises. She wants to take $50,000 from her 401(k) while continuing to work. Her CPA reviews the plan document and confirms it permits in-service distributions. Apply the tax rules to determine the consequences of this distribution.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The distribution is prohibited because she has not separated from service","The distribution is taxable as ordinary income with no 10% penalty","The distribution is tax-free because she is over 59½","The distribution is subject to both ordinary income tax and a 10% penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 401(k)(2)(B)(i)(IV) permits in-service distributions from 401(k) plans after age 59½ if the plan document allows it. Since Tanya is 61, the distribution is permitted and taxable as ordinary income. The 10% early withdrawal penalty under Section 72(t) does not apply because she is over 59½. Wrong (A): In-service distributions are allowed after age 59½ if the plan permits them; separation from service is not required. Wrong (C): Distributions from a traditional 401(k) are always taxable as ordinary income regardless of age. Wrong (D): The 10% penalty does not apply to distributions after age 59½.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Top-heavy plan rules (60% key employees)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Brighton Financial's 401(k) plan has $2,000,000 in total accrued benefits. Key employees (as defined under IRC Section 416(i)) hold $1,300,000 of that total. The plan administrator must apply the top-heavy rules. What is the consequence of this determination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No special rules apply because the plan is not top-heavy","Key employees must forfeit the excess above 60%","The employer must freeze all contributions to key employees","The employer must make a minimum contribution of 3% of compensation for non-key employees"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 416(g), a plan is top-heavy if more than 60% of accrued benefits belong to key employees. Here, $1,300,000 / $2,000,000 = 65%, exceeding 60%. Under Section 416(c), the employer must provide a minimum contribution of 3% of compensation (or the highest key employee contribution rate, if less) for each non-key employee participant. Wrong (A): The plan IS top-heavy because key employees hold 65% of accrued benefits, exceeding the 60% threshold. Wrong (B): Key employees do not forfeit balances; the remedy is minimum contributions to non-key employees, not clawbacks. Wrong (C): There is no rule requiring a freeze on key employee contributions; the plan must simply provide minimum contributions to non-key employees.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Education savings bonds (Series EE/I) income exclusion with phaseout
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Diane Mercer advises married couple filing jointly with MAGI of $155,000 in 2026. They redeemed $20,000 in Series EE savings bonds (purchased after 1989) with $6,000 in accrued interest. They paid $18,000 in qualified higher education expenses for their child. The 2026 MFJ income phase-out for the education savings bond exclusion begins at $148,450 and ends at $178,450. Evaluate the excludable interest amount.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$5,400 — full exclusion of the qualified portion","$4,227 — partial exclusion after applying both the expense ratio and income phase-out","$6,000 — full interest exclusion because expenses exceed half the redemption","$0 — the couple's income exceeds the phase-out threshold entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 135, the exclusion is calculated in two steps. First, the expense ratio: $18,000 / $20,000 = 90%, so excludable interest before phase-out = $6,000 x 90% = $5,400. Second, the income phase-out: excess MAGI = $155,000 - $148,450 = $6,550; phase-out range = $30,000; reduction = $6,550 / $30,000 = 21.83%. Excludable amount = $5,400 x (1 - 0.2183) = $4,222, approximately $4,227. Wrong (A): $5,400 ignores the income phase-out reduction, which applies because MAGI exceeds the threshold. Wrong (C): The full $6,000 cannot be excluded because expenses ($18,000) are less than the total redemption ($20,000) and income is within the phase-out range. Wrong (D): The couple's MAGI of $155,000 is within the phase-out range, not above it; the exclusion is reduced but not eliminated.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — QJSA requirements for married participants
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Married participant Vincent Russo, age 65, is retiring from Cascade Industries and wants to receive his $400,000 defined benefit pension as a single-life annuity to maximize his monthly payment. His spouse, Maria, would receive nothing after his death. The plan administrator must determine whether this election is valid.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Vincent can elect the single-life annuity without any further requirements","Vincent can elect the single-life annuity only if Maria provides written, notarized consent to waive the QJSA","The plan must pay a qualified joint and survivor annuity regardless of Vincent's preference","Maria must be at least age 62 to consent to a QJSA waiver"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 417(a)(2), the default distribution form for married participants in defined benefit plans is a qualified joint and survivor annuity (QJSA). To waive the QJSA and elect a single-life annuity, the spouse must provide written consent witnessed by a plan representative or notary public. Wrong (A): A married participant cannot unilaterally waive the QJSA; spousal consent is required under Section 417. Wrong (C): The QJSA is the default, but participants may waive it with proper spousal consent. Wrong (D): There is no minimum age requirement for spousal consent to a QJSA waiver.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — ABLE accounts for disabled individuals
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is the purpose of an ABLE (Achieving a Better Life Experience) account under IRC Section 529A?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To provide tax-free education savings exclusively for college expenses","To allow individuals with disabilities to save for qualified disability expenses without losing public benefits","To replace Social Security disability benefits with private savings","To fund employer-sponsored disability insurance plans"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 529A establishes ABLE accounts, which allow eligible individuals with disabilities (onset before age 26) to save for qualified disability expenses — including housing, transportation, health care, and education — without jeopardizing eligibility for means-tested public benefits like SSI and Medicaid. Wrong (A): ABLE accounts cover a broad range of disability-related expenses, not just college; 529 plans are the education-specific vehicle. Wrong (C): ABLE accounts supplement, not replace, public benefits like SSDI and SSI. Wrong (D): ABLE accounts are individual savings accounts, not employer-sponsored insurance plans.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — One-rollover-per-year IRA rule
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is the IRS rule regarding indirect (60-day) rollovers between IRAs?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A taxpayer may perform unlimited indirect rollovers per year","A taxpayer is limited to one indirect IRA-to-IRA rollover per 12-month period","Indirect rollovers are prohibited; only direct trustee-to-trustee transfers are allowed","A taxpayer may perform one indirect rollover per IRA account per year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 408(d)(3)(B), a taxpayer is limited to one indirect (60-day) IRA-to-IRA rollover in any 12-month period across all IRAs. This rule was clarified in 2014 to apply on an aggregate basis. Direct trustee-to-trustee transfers are unlimited and not subject to this rule. Wrong (A): The IRS limits indirect rollovers to one per 12-month period; unlimited rollovers would create opportunities for short-term tax-free borrowing. Wrong (C): Indirect rollovers are permitted but limited; they are not prohibited. Wrong (D): The one-rollover rule applies per taxpayer across all IRAs, not per individual IRA account.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Roth conversion ordering rules and multiple-year conversions
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Amanda Foley advises client Derek Lane, age 52, who converted $40,000 from a traditional IRA to a Roth IRA in 2020 (paying tax on the full amount) and another $30,000 in 2024. Derek has also made $14,000 in direct Roth IRA contributions over the years. In 2026, Derek withdraws $60,000 from his Roth IRA. Evaluate the tax and penalty consequences of the withdrawal using the Roth ordering rules.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$60,000 is entirely tax-free and penalty-free because total contributions and conversions exceed the withdrawal","$14,000 is tax-free; $40,000 is penalty-free (2020 conversion met 5-year rule); $6,000 from the 2024 conversion incurs a 10% penalty","$14,000 is tax-free; $46,000 is subject to ordinary income tax and 10% penalty","$60,000 is tax-free because all conversions were previously taxed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 408A(d)(4), Roth IRA distributions follow ordering rules: (1) contributions first (tax-free, penalty-free), (2) conversions in chronological order (tax-free since already taxed, but 10% penalty if within 5 years and under 59½). Derek's $14,000 in contributions comes out first — no tax, no penalty. Next, the 2020 conversion ($40,000): the 5-year period (2020-2024) is satisfied, so no penalty. The remaining $6,000 comes from the 2024 conversion, which is within its 5-year period and Derek is under 59½, so a 10% penalty of $600 applies. No income tax applies to any portion because conversions were previously taxed. Wrong (A): The 2024 conversion has not met the 5-year holding period, so the 10% penalty applies to that portion. Wrong (C): Conversion amounts are not subject to income tax again; they were fully taxed at conversion. Wrong (D): Although conversions were taxed, the 5-year holding period for penalty-free treatment must be evaluated separately for each conversion year.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Highly compensated employee (HCE) definition
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Brookstone Partners is running 401(k) nondiscrimination testing for 2026. Employee Angela Torres earned $160,000 in 2025 and owns no company stock. The HCE compensation threshold for the look-back year is $155,000. How is Angela classified for testing purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Non-highly compensated employee because the threshold is $160,000","Highly compensated employee because she earned more than $155,000 in the prior year","Highly compensated employee only if she is also a 5% owner","Non-highly compensated employee because the threshold applies to the current year's compensation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 414(q)(1)(B), an employee is a highly compensated employee if they received more than the indexed threshold ($155,000 for 2025 look-back year) in compensation during the preceding year. Angela earned $160,000, exceeding the threshold. Wrong (A): The 2025 HCE threshold is $155,000, not $160,000. Wrong (C): HCE status is determined by either compensation exceeding the threshold OR being a 5% owner; both are independent tests under Section 414(q). Wrong (D): The compensation test uses the prior year (look-back year), not the current plan year.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — In-plan Roth conversion (designated Roth account)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Employee Shawn Peters, age 40, has $120,000 in pre-tax contributions in his employer's 401(k). The plan offers an in-plan Roth conversion feature. Shawn converts $50,000 from his pre-tax 401(k) account to the plan's designated Roth account. Apply the tax rules to determine the consequences of this conversion.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $50,000 is tax-free because it stays within the same plan","The $50,000 is included in gross income and a new 5-year holding period begins for the converted amount","The $50,000 is subject to a 10% early withdrawal penalty in addition to income tax","Shawn must wait until age 59½ to perform an in-plan Roth conversion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 402A(c)(4), an in-plan Roth conversion (also called an in-plan Roth rollover) requires the converted pre-tax amount to be included in gross income in the year of conversion. A separate 5-year holding period begins for the converted amount. No 10% early withdrawal penalty applies because the funds remain in the plan. Wrong (A): Converting pre-tax dollars to a Roth account is a taxable event regardless of whether the funds remain in the same plan. Wrong (C): The 10% penalty does not apply to in-plan Roth conversions because no distribution occurs; funds stay within the plan. Wrong (D): In-plan Roth conversions are available at any age if the plan document permits them; there is no age 59½ requirement.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Employer deduction limit for plan contributions
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Harlow Industries has total eligible payroll of $2,000,000 in 2026. The company contributes $600,000 to its 401(k) profit-sharing plan. Under IRC Section 404(a), what is the maximum deductible employer contribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$500,000","$600,000","$400,000","$350,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 404(a)(3) limits the employer's deductible contribution to a profit-sharing or 401(k) plan to 25% of total eligible compensation. 25% of $2,000,000 = $500,000. The excess $100,000 is not deductible in 2026 but may be carried forward. Wrong (B): The full $600,000 exceeds the 25% deduction limit and cannot be fully deducted. Wrong (C): $400,000 represents 20% of payroll, understating the 25% limit. Wrong (D): $350,000 is the individual compensation cap for plan purposes, not the employer deduction limit.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Coverdell ESA broader K-12 expenses vs 529
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$The Rivera family uses both a 529 plan and a Coverdell ESA for their 10-year-old daughter attending private school. In 2026, they pay $10,000 tuition, $1,200 for school uniforms, and $800 for after-school tutoring. They want to determine which account should pay each expense to maximize tax-free treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Use the 529 for all expenses since it covers all K-12 costs","Use the Coverdell ESA for uniforms and tutoring; use the 529 for tuition up to $10,000","Use the 529 for tuition only; the uniforms and tutoring are not qualified expenses for either account","Use the Coverdell ESA for all expenses since it has no K-12 restrictions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Coverdell ESAs under IRC Section 530(b)(4) define qualified elementary and secondary expenses broadly, including tuition, uniforms, tutoring, and supplies. Section 529 plans are limited to tuition only for K-12 (up to $10,000/year). The optimal strategy uses the Coverdell for uniforms ($1,200) and tutoring ($800), and the 529 for the $10,000 tuition. Wrong (A): 529 plans cover only K-12 tuition, not uniforms or tutoring at the K-12 level. Wrong (C): Uniforms and tutoring are qualified K-12 expenses under the Coverdell ESA, even though they are not qualified for 529 plans. Wrong (D): While the Coverdell has broader K-12 coverage, its $2,000 annual contribution limit makes it insufficient for all expenses; the 529 is better for the larger tuition amount.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Excess deferral 402(g) correction deadline
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is the deadline for correcting an excess 401(k) elective deferral under IRC Section 402(g)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["December 31 of the year the excess occurred","March 15 of the following year","April 15 of the following year (tax filing deadline)","No correction is required; the excess is automatically forfeited"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 402(g)(2), excess elective deferrals must be distributed (with allocable income) by April 15 of the following year to avoid double taxation. If not corrected by this deadline, the excess is taxed in the year of deferral and again when distributed. Wrong (A): December 31 is not the correction deadline; the taxpayer has until April 15 of the following year. Wrong (B): March 15 is the deadline for correcting ADP/ACP test failures, not excess deferrals under Section 402(g). Wrong (D): Excess deferrals are not automatically forfeited; the participant must request a corrective distribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — 529 plan estate tax treatment (removal from estate)
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Grandmother Patricia Wen contributes $19,000 to a 529 plan for her grandson in 2026 (the exact annual gift tax exclusion amount). She retains the ability to change the beneficiary and withdraw funds. Her estate planning attorney asks whether the contribution is removed from Patricia's taxable estate. Analyze the estate tax treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $19,000 is included in Patricia's estate because she retained control over the account","The $19,000 is excluded from her estate because it qualifies as a completed gift for gift tax purposes","The $19,000 is included only if Patricia actually changes the beneficiary before death","Half the contribution is included because of the retained withdrawal right"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 529(c)(2), contributions to a 529 plan are treated as completed gifts for gift and estate tax purposes, removing them from the donor's taxable estate. This is true even though the account owner retains the ability to change beneficiaries or withdraw funds — a unique exception to the normal retained-interest rules. Wrong (A): Despite the retained control, Section 529 provides a special rule treating contributions as completed gifts excluded from the estate. Wrong (C): Inclusion is not contingent on actually changing the beneficiary; the question is determined by the statute at the time of death. Wrong (D): The full contribution is excluded; there is no partial-inclusion rule for 529 contributions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Keogh (HR-10) plan for self-employed
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What type of individual may establish a Keogh (HR-10) retirement plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any W-2 employee of a corporation","A self-employed individual or unincorporated business owner","Only taxpayers with AGI below $150,000","Only individuals who do not participate in any other retirement plan"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Keogh plans (also called HR-10 plans) are qualified retirement plans established by self-employed individuals and unincorporated business owners (sole proprietors and partners). They may be structured as defined benefit or defined contribution plans. Wrong (A): W-2 employees of corporations participate in employer-sponsored plans, not Keogh plans. Wrong (C): There is no AGI limitation for establishing a Keogh plan. Wrong (D): Self-employed individuals may participate in a Keogh plan even if they also participate in other retirement plans.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Pooled employer plans (PEP) under SECURE Act
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Three unrelated small businesses — a dental practice, a consulting firm, and a retail shop — want to reduce 401(k) administration costs. Their CPA recommends joining a pooled employer plan (PEP) under the SECURE Act. One business has compliance concerns. Apply the PEP rules to determine how a compliance failure by one employer affects the others.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All three employers are disqualified if one fails compliance testing","Each employer's portion is tested separately; one employer's failure does not disqualify the others","The PEP is automatically terminated upon any compliance failure","The pooled plan provider absorbs all compliance penalties on behalf of participating employers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SECURE Act Section 101, pooled employer plans (PEPs) include the "bad apple" protection rule. Each participating employer's plan segment is treated separately for qualification purposes, so one employer's compliance failure does not jeopardize the qualified status of other employers' plans within the PEP. Wrong (A): The "bad apple" rule specifically prevents disqualification of the entire PEP due to one employer's failure. Wrong (C): Automatic termination is not required; the noncompliant employer may be removed while the PEP continues. Wrong (D): The pooled plan provider manages administration but does not absorb all compliance penalties; each employer retains responsibility for its own fiduciary obligations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L2 — 529 to ABLE account rollover
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$The Watson family has $40,000 remaining in a 529 plan for their son, who has a qualifying disability. They learn that 529 funds can be rolled to an ABLE account. What is the annual rollover limit from a 529 plan to an ABLE account?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$40,000 — the full balance can be rolled at once","The ABLE account annual contribution limit (including all other contributions for the year)","$10,000 lifetime limit","There is no limit on 529-to-ABLE rollovers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 529A(c)(1)(C), rollovers from a 529 plan to an ABLE account for the same beneficiary (or a family member) are permitted, but the rollover counts toward the ABLE account's annual contribution limit. The total of all contributions and rollovers to the ABLE account cannot exceed the annual limit in a given year. Wrong (A): The full $40,000 cannot be rolled at once because ABLE accounts have annual contribution limits. Wrong (C): There is no $10,000 lifetime cap on 529-to-ABLE rollovers; the constraint is the annual contribution limit. Wrong (D): Rollovers are subject to the ABLE annual contribution limit, so they are not unlimited.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2)
;

COMMIT;
